<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of La Forbie</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1244">1244</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Hiribya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of La Forbie was fought on 17–18 October 1244 near Gaza between the army of the Ayyubid sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, as-Salih Ayyub, with Khwarazmian allies under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a>, and an alliance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader states</a> with the Ayyubid rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a>. The Egyptian victory destroyed the last large Crusader field army and confirmed Muslim control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmians">Khwarazmians</a> had sacked that summer.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-la-forbie.jpg" alt="Battle of La Forbie" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matthew Paris · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Forbie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> by the Khwarezmians in August 1244 alarmed Christian and Muslim states alike. Al-Mansur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a>, and an-Nasir Dawud of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-karak">Kerak</a> joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Templars</a>, the Hospitallers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>, the Order of Saint Lazarus and the remaining forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> against the Egyptian sultanate. The armies met near Forbie, a small village north-east of Gaza. Overall Christian command lay with Walter IV of Brienne, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a>, with Robert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a>, Patriarch of Jerusalem, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-of-montfort">Philip of Montfort</a>, Constable of Jerusalem, also present.</p><p>The Christians fielded about 1,000 cavalry and 6,000 foot soldiers, Al-Mansur about 2,000 cavalry with troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, and the Transjordanian contingent about 2,000 mounted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a>. The Egyptian army, led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> officer Rukn al-Din <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> al-Salihi, was slightly weaker. Al-Mansur advised a fortified defensive camp, expecting the undisciplined Khwarezmians to disperse, but Walter would not refuse battle while he held the rare advantage of numbers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 17 October the Christian knights charged repeatedly along the line, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> held. The next morning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> threw the Khwarezmians against the Damascene centre, which broke, and they then cut the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> on the left to pieces. Al-Mansur's cavalry stood firm until nearly destroyed, and he left the field with 280 survivors. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> on the right charged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> and at first drove them back, but the Khwarezmians attacked their rear and flanks, and after several hours their resistance collapsed. Over 5,000 Crusaders died and 800 were captured, among them Walter of Brienne; only 33 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Templars</a>, 27 Hospitallers and three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> survived, and the Master of the Temple, Armand de Périgord, was killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iv">Pope Innocent IV</a> called for the Seventh Crusade at the First Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> in 1245, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> never again mustered major power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> had not put so large an army into the field since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a> and could never again undertake offensive operations. The victory brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> no lasting gain: the Khwarezmians fell out with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and were defeated near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mansur-ibrahim">Al-Mansur Ibrahim</a> in 1246, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a>, accused of siding with them, was arrested by as-Salih Ayyub and died in prison.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Forbie” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Richard I of England’s victory at Arsuf in 1191 helped restore the coast, but another field army was destroyed by the Khwarazmians and Ayyubids at <b>La Forbie</b> in 1244.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His successor al-Mansur Ibrahim fought alongside the Franks against Egypt and lost at <b>La Forbie</b> in 1244.</blockquote><a href="/state/ayyubid-emirate-of-homs">Ayyubid Emirate of Homs</a></li></ul></section>
